{"prompt": "I failed my driving test for the third time and I feel like an idiot.", "response": "Three attempts doesn't make you an idiot — it makes you someone who keeps showing up, which is genuinely hard. Plenty of great drivers needed several tries; the test measures nerves as much as skill. Be gentle with yourself today. When you're ready, ask your examiner's feedback sheet what specifically to practice, and take a lesson focused just on that."}
{"prompt": "My cat died last week and I can't stop feeling guilty that I didn't notice she was sick sooner.", "response": "I'm so sorry. That guilt is a sign of how much you loved her — but cats are famously good at hiding illness; even vets often catch things late. You gave her a home where she felt safe her whole life. Grief takes the time it takes, and it's okay to miss her out loud."}
{"prompt": "I started trying to learn guitar again after 20 years and my fingers hurt and I keep making mistakes. Should I just quit while I'm ahead?", "response": "Your fingers hurting is actually a good sign — it means you're doing something new and your body is adapting. Everyone who plays guitar went through this, even the ones who look effortlessly skilled now. Calluses will form in a week or two and the pain will fade. For now, try shorter practice sessions, maybe 15 minutes a day instead of longer stretches. Be patient with yourself; you're relearning, and that takes time. The fact that you picked it up again at all shows you love music, and that alone is worth celebrating."}
{"prompt": "I've been working 60-hour weeks for months and I'm completely burnt out. I can't sleep, I snap at my family, and I dread every Monday. I know I need to change something but I'm terrified of losing my job.", "response": "What you're describing sounds exhausting, and I want to validate that your feelings make complete sense — anyone would feel this way under sustained pressure like that. Your health matters more than any single job. Have you explored whether your employer offers any flexibility? Even small changes like a four-day week, remote work a couple days, or adjusting your hours could make a big difference. If that's not possible, you might also consider talking to someone — a therapist or even an employee assistance program if your company has one. You don't have to navigate this alone, and taking care of yourself isn't selfish; it's essential."}
{"prompt": "I just moved to a new city for work and I have no friends here. Every weekend I come home to an empty apartment and I feel really lonely. Everyone else seems to have a social life except me.", "response": "Moving to a new place is genuinely hard, and that loneliness you're feeling is completely normal — it's your brain telling you that humans need connection. The good news is that building a social circle takes time, and most people in your position feel exactly the same way you do right now. Start small: try joining a local group related to something you enjoy, whether it's a running club, book group, or volunteer organization. Apps like Meetup can help you find people nearby. It usually takes about three months of showing up consistently before you start forming real connections. Be gentle with yourself during this transition; you're doing something brave by starting fresh in a new place."}
{"prompt": "I accidentally deleted an important file at work and my boss was furious. I've been replaying it in my head all day and I feel like such an incompetent person.", "response": "Making mistakes is part of being human, especially in a role where you're trusted with important work. One mistake doesn't define your entire professional worth — your boss was upset about the situation, not about you as a person. Take a breath and focus on the practical next steps: is there a backup you can restore? Have you learned anything about preventing this in the future? You might consider apologizing briefly and moving on, but don't keep beating yourself up. Even the most experienced professionals have had 'oops' moments. What matters most is how you handle it afterward, and the fact that you care this much shows you're conscientious."}
{"prompt": "I have a job interview tomorrow and I'm so nervous I can barely sleep. What if I freeze up and say something stupid? What if they don't like me?", "response": "Interview nerves are incredibly common — even people who've done hundreds of interviews still get butterflies. That anxiety you're feeling? It's actually a sign that this opportunity matters to you, and there's nothing wrong with that. Tonight, try to do something calming: maybe a warm bath, some light stretching, or watching something funny to take your mind off things. Tomorrow, remember that they invited you because they're interested in you — you've already passed some hurdles. It's okay to pause and think before answering questions. And if you make a small mistake, nobody will judge you for it. You're going to do fine, and either way, this is just one of many opportunities ahead of you."}
